"<div><div class=\" response-message\"></div></div><div><div class=\" response-message\"><h2>Edge Cases and Error States</h2>\n<p>✅ Edge case analysis complete</p>\n<p>This analysis provides a formal state machine model for a <strong>User Authentication Flow</strong> within a Web Application Security context, followed by a rigorous edge-case and error-condition analysis.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Part 1: Finite State Machine Model</h3>\n<h4>1. Logged Out</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> The default state where no user session exists.</li>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> Initial / Normal</li>\n<li><strong>Invariants:</strong> No session token exists in the client; <code>currentUser</code> is null.</li>\n<li><strong>Entry Conditions:</strong> Application start, explicit logout, or session termination.</li>\n<li><strong>Exit Conditions:</strong> User submits login credentials.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>2. Authenticating</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> A transient state where the system validates primary credentials (username/password).</li>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> Normal (Transient)</li>\n<li><strong>Invariants:</strong> Request is in flight; UI is likely showing a loading spinner.</li>\n<li><strong>Entry Conditions:</strong> <code>submit_login</code> event triggered from <em>Logged Out</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Exit Conditions:</strong> Credentials valid (move to <em>MFA Pending</em> or <em>Logged In</em>), credentials invalid (move to <em>Logged Out</em> with error), or account locked.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>3. MFA Pending</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> Primary credentials are correct, but the system requires a second factor (TOTP, SMS, etc.).</li>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> Normal (Transient)</li>\n<li><strong>Invariants:</strong> Primary identity is verified; session is &quot;partially&quot; authenticated (limited scope).</li>\n<li><strong>Entry Conditions:</strong> Successful credential validation where MFA is enabled.</li>\n<li><strong>Exit Conditions:</strong> Valid MFA code provided, MFA bypass/recovery used, or MFA timeout.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>4. Logged In</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> The user is fully authenticated and has access to protected resources.</li>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> Normal / Stable</li>\n<li><strong>Invariants:</strong> Valid session token exists; <code>currentUser</code> is populated; session expiry timer is active.</li>\n<li><strong>Entry Conditions:</strong> Successful primary authentication (if MFA disabled) or successful MFA verification.</li>\n<li><strong>Exit Conditions:</strong> User logs out, session expires, or account is administratively disabled.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>5. Password Reset Required</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> User is authenticated but must change their password before proceeding (e.g., first-time login or forced reset).</li>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> Normal (Transient)</li>\n<li><strong>Invariants:</strong> User identity is known; access to all other features is blocked by a middleware interceptor.</li>\n<li><strong>Entry Conditions:</strong> Successful login but <code>password_change_flag</code> is true.</li>\n<li><strong>Exit Conditions:</strong> Successful password update (moves to <em>Logged In</em>).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>6. Account Locked</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> The account is temporarily or permanently disabled due to security policy violations.</li>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> Error / Stable</li>\n<li><strong>Invariants:</strong> Authentication attempts are rejected regardless of credential validity.</li>\n<li><strong>Entry Conditions:</strong> Exceeding maximum login attempts; detected suspicious activity.</li>\n<li><strong>Exit Conditions:</strong> Administrative unlock or expiration of lockout duration.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>7. Session Expired</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> The session token is no longer valid due to inactivity or absolute timeout.</li>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> Error / Terminal</li>\n<li><strong>Invariants:</strong> Previous session token is rejected by the server.</li>\n<li><strong>Entry Conditions:</strong> Current time &gt; <code>session_expiry_time</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Exit Conditions:</strong> Redirect to <em>Logged Out</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h3>Part 2: Edge Case and Error Analysis</h3>\n<h4>1. Invalid Transitions</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Direct Access to &quot;Logged In&quot;:</strong> Attempting to transition from <em>Logged Out</em> directly to <em>Logged In</em> by manually setting a local storage token.\n<ul>\n<li><em>Mitigation:</em> Server-side validation of every request; tokens must be cryptographically signed.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>MFA Bypass:</strong> Attempting to transition from <em>MFA Pending</em> to <em>Logged In</em> by calling a protected API endpoint without the MFA verification step.\n<ul>\n<li><em>Mitigation:</em> Implement &quot;Partial Session&quot; scopes where the token issued after step 1 only permits the MFA verification route.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>2. Missing Transitions</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The &quot;Back Button&quot; Problem:</strong> User is in <em>MFA Pending</em> and clicks the browser back button.\n<ul>\n<li><em>Analysis:</em> Does the system return to <em>Logged Out</em>? If it stays in <em>MFA Pending</em>, a different user could potentially see the previous user's email/phone hint.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Password Reset during MFA:</strong> If a user forgets their MFA device while in the <em>MFA Pending</em> state.\n<ul>\n<li><em>Analysis:</em> There must be a transition from <em>MFA Pending</em> to a <em>Recovery/Reset</em> flow.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>3. Error States</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Clock Skew Error:</strong> The client's clock is significantly different from the server's, causing immediate <em>Session Expired</em> states.</li>\n<li><strong>Upstream Identity Provider (IdP) Down:</strong> If using OAuth/SAML, the system enters an undefined state if the callback fails.\n<ul>\n<li><em>Recommendation:</em> Create a specific <em>External Auth Failure</em> state to handle 5xx errors from providers like Google or Okta.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>4. Recovery Paths</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>From Account Locked:</strong> Provide a &quot;Forgot Password&quot; path that triggers an email-based identity verification to unlock the account.</li>\n<li><strong>From Session Expired:</strong> Implement &quot;Graceful Re-authentication.&quot; Instead of clearing all user data, show a modal to re-enter credentials to resume the previous state (preserving unsaved work).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>5. Boundary Conditions</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Simultaneous Expiry and Action:</strong> User clicks &quot;Submit&quot; at the exact millisecond the session expires.\n<ul>\n<li><em>Handling:</em> The server must return a specific 401 sub-code that the client interprets to save the request payload in local storage, re-auth, and retry.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Maximum MFA Attempts:</strong> What happens after 3 failed TOTP attempts?\n<ul>\n<li><em>Handling:</em> Transition to <em>Account Locked</em> or force a return to <em>Logged Out</em> with a cooldown.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>6. Race Conditions</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Double-Click Login:</strong> User clicks &quot;Login&quot; twice rapidly.\n<ul>\n<li><em>Risk:</em> Two authentication requests are sent. If the first succeeds and the second is processed slightly later, it might invalidate the first session or cause &quot;Concurrent Session&quot; errors.</li>\n<li><em>Mitigation:</em> Disable the login button immediately upon entering the <em>Authenticating</em> state (Idempotency keys).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Concurrent Logins:</strong> User logs in on Chrome and Firefox simultaneously.\n<ul>\n<li><em>Risk:</em> If the security policy is &quot;Single Active Session,&quot; the first session may be killed while the user is mid-transaction.</li>\n<li><em>Mitigation:</em> Implement a <em>Session Conflict</em> state where the user is asked which session to terminate.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Summary of Recommendations</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>State Scoping:</strong> Ensure the <em>MFA Pending</em> state uses a restricted-scope token that cannot access business logic APIs.</li>\n<li><strong>Explicit Timeouts:</strong> Every transient state (<em>Authenticating</em>, <em>MFA Pending</em>) must have a hard timeout to prevent &quot;hanging&quot; states.</li>\n<li><strong>Idempotency:</strong> Use request IDs for the login flow to prevent race conditions during high latency.</li>\n<li><strong>Global Error Handler:</strong> Implement a &quot;Catch-All&quot; state for unexpected API responses (e.g., 502 Bad Gateway) to ensure the UI doesn't get stuck in a loading spinner.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></div><div><div class=\"spinner-border\" role=\"status\"><span class=\"sr-only\">Loading...</span></div></div>"